<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Częstochowa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Częstochowa is a city in the Silesian Voivodeship of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a> river. It is famous for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jasna-gora-monastery">Jasna Góra Monastery</a>, founded in 1382 by Pauline monks, which houses the icon of the Black Madonna and is the main Catholic pilgrimage site in Poland. The city later grew into an industrial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/czestochowa.jpg" alt="Częstochowa" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jerzy Szota · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cz%C4%99stochowa_klasztor_Jasna_G%C3%B3ra-2162.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Częstochowa lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a> river in the Kraków-Częstochowa Upland. Although it is now in the Silesian Voivodeship, it is historically part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a>, and before the Partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1795 it belonged to the Kraków Voivodeship. Its name means Częstoch's place and comes from a personal name found in medieval documents also as Częstobor and Częstomir. A Lusatian culture cemetery of about 750-550 BCE in the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakow-swietokrzyskie">Raków</a> is now an archaeological reserve with a museum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeology shows the first medieval settlement was founded in the late 11th century, in Piast-ruled Poland. It first appears in a document of 1220, in which Bishop Iwo Odrowąż of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> listed the properties of the Mstów monastery; two villages are named, Częstochowa and Częstochówka, the latter on the hill where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jasna-gora-monastery">Jasna Góra Monastery</a> was later built. The town prospered in the late 15th and early 16th centuries through the efforts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a>, who often passed through on his way to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a>. It received the right to collect tolls on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a> bridge in 1504 and the right to hold one fair a year in 1508, raised to three in 1564 and six in 1639.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> seized the city in 1793 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition of Poland</a>. It joined the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in 1807 and Russian-controlled Congress Poland in 1815. In 1809 Austrians unsuccessfully besieged the monastery, and in 1813 the Russians took Jasna Góra after a two-week siege and razed its fortifications. Under German occupation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the city lost its Russian markets, and about 20,000 people left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a> and other German provinces. In 1919-1921 it was a centre of support for the Silesian Uprisings.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> occupied Częstochowa on 3 September 1939 and incorporated it into the General Government. On 4 September, called Bloody Monday, German troops killed 227 people in the city, 205 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and 22 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>; some estimates put the number above 1,000. After the war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> mill was greatly expanded, and the city became an industrial and academic centre. It was the seat of its own voivodeship from 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Częstochowa has 214,342 inhabitants, making it the thirteenth-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and is the largest economic and cultural hub of the northern part of the Silesian Voivodeship. Millions of pilgrims come each year to see the Black Madonna at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jasna-gora-monastery">Jasna Góra Monastery</a>. It is the seat of a Roman Catholic archdiocese. The steelworks, first established in 1896, remain among the largest in Poland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Częstochowa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With their position in southern Lesser Poland deteriorating, the Swedish invaders lifted their siege of the Pauline monastery of Jasna Góra in <b>Częstochowa</b>, home of the Black Madonna icon.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-27">27 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Władysław II of Opole served as palatine of Hungary and in 1382 founded the Pauline monastery of Jasna Góra at <b>Częstochowa</b>, bringing the Black Madonna icon.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-opole">Duchy of Opole</a></li></ul></section>
